            "<strong>Nutrition screening</strong> on admission and weekly identifies the patient whose malnutrition will slow healing, weaken the cough and deepen the pressure injury before any clinician has noticed it. The Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool used in most adult wards scores three things: body-mass index (score 2 if below 18.5, 1 if 18.5–20), <strong>unplanned weight loss in the past three to six months</strong> (score 2 if more than 10 per cent, 1 if 5–10 per cent) and the <strong>acute disease effect</strong> (score 2 if the patient has eaten nothing or is likely to eat nothing for more than five days); a total of 0 is low risk (repeat weekly), 1 medium (record intake for three days and reassess), <strong>2 or more high risk</strong> (dietitian referral, a treatment plan with supplements or tube feeding, and intake recorded). Where height and weight cannot be measured, mid-upper arm circumference below 23.5 cm suggests a body-mass index below 20 and a clear history of loose clothing and rings counts as weight loss. In children the screen is the weight-for-age and weight-for-height plot, the mid-upper arm circumference and the check for bilateral pitting oedema taught in the vital-signs lesson, and a child with severe acute malnutrition and any complication is admitted to a stabilisation centre and fed F-75 under the national protocol, never a normal ward diet.",
            "<strong>The fluid balance chart</strong> is the record of every measured millilitre in and out over twenty-four hours, and it is only as accurate as the least careful entry. <strong>Intake</strong>: oral fluids measured in a marked jug and cup (the ward's cups and bowls are calibrated and the volumes displayed — a cup of tea is not ‘200 mL’ because that is what the last nurse wrote), enteral feeds and flushes, every intravenous infusion and bolus, blood products, and the volume of intravenous medicines. <strong>Output</strong>: urine measured in a jug or an hourly urometer (never estimated from the bag's markings alone), vomit and nasogastric aspirate, drain and stoma output, liquid stool (measured or estimated with the number of episodes), wound exudate from weighed dressings, and in the incontinent patient weighed pads (one gram of weight gain is one millilitre). Insensible loss through skin and lungs — about 500–800 mL a day in an adult and much more with fever (about 10 per cent more for each degree), tachypnoea, burns, phototherapy and a hot ward — is not written on the chart but is remembered when the balance is interpreted. Totals are calculated at the end of every shift and at twenty-four hours, and the balance is read against the <strong>daily weight</strong>, which is the most accurate measure of fluid change, and against the <strong>minimum acceptable urine output of 0.5 mL/kg/hour in an adult and 1 mL/kg/hour in a child</strong> — an output below this for two consecutive hours is reported, as is a positive balance of more than two litres in a patient who is not bleeding, a negative balance in a patient who is vomiting, and any output that is bloody, cola-coloured or absent. A chart that shows input and no output, or round numbers in every box, is a record that the fluids were charted, not that the patient was measured; the critically ill, the post-operative, the patient on diuretics or intravenous fluids, the patient with heart or kidney failure, with diarrhoea, with a drain or with pre-eclampsia is on a chart until the doctor stops it, and the patient is told why her jug matters.",
