            "<strong>Purpose.</strong> Helping a patient to pass urine or stool in bed or at the bedside, promptly and privately, protects continence, skin and dignity and prevents the falls that happen when a patient tries to reach the toilet alone. The commode is used in preference to the bedpan for anyone who can be helped to sit, because the sitting position empties the bladder and bowel more completely and with less strain; the bedpan is for the patient who cannot sit out; the <strong>slipper (fracture) pan</strong>, flat end under the buttocks, is for the patient who cannot lift the pelvis — a hip fracture, a spinal injury, immediately after hip surgery. <strong>Steps.</strong> Respond to the request <strong>immediately</strong> — the wait is the commonest cause of incontinence and falls on a ward; screen the bed; hand hygiene, apron and gloves; warm a metal pan with warm water and dry it, or use a disposable pulp pan on its support; raise the head of the bed unless contraindicated, ask the patient to flex the knees and lift the hips while you slide the pan under with the curved edge towards the back, or roll the patient onto her side, place the pan against the buttocks and roll her back onto it; check that she is sitting squarely; give toilet paper and the call bell and leave if it is safe to do so, staying within earshot; remove the pan by reversing the steps, holding it steady, and cover it; give perineal hygiene front to back and offer hand-washing to the patient; inspect, measure or test the contents before disposing of them in the bedpan washer, macerator or sluice (never the hand basin), and clean and store reusable pans; record the volume and character. A patient is never left on a bedpan for more than a few minutes — the rim creates a pressure injury — and a man is given a urinal with the penis placed inside it and the bottle supported, not left balanced between the legs. The commode has its brakes on, the patient's feet reach the floor, the call bell is in reach and the pan is removed, covered and emptied immediately afterwards.",
            "<strong>Dipstick urinalysis</strong> on a fresh specimen (within an hour): all pads wetted, the strip withdrawn edge-wise along the rim, held horizontally, and each pad read against the chart <strong>at the printed time</strong> in good light with the container closed at once. <strong>Nitrite with leucocytes</strong> suggests infection and is sent for culture; nitrite alone is specific but insensitive. <strong>Protein</strong> in pregnancy is a blood pressure now (pre-eclampsia) and persistent proteinuria elsewhere is a kidney referral. <strong>Glucose</strong> is a capillary glucose now; <strong>glucose with ketones</strong> in an unwell patient is diabetic ketoacidosis until proved otherwise. <strong>Blood</strong> is a stone, infection, tumour, schistosomiasis in the endemic regions, menstruation or a catheter trauma, and persistent microscopic haematuria is referred. <strong>Specific gravity</strong> above 1.030 is dehydration; a pH persistently alkaline suggests a urea-splitting infection. Colour, clarity and smell are recorded alongside — cloudy and offensive, tea- or cola-coloured, frank blood or clots, or the deep orange of rifampicin or dehydration. A dipstick in an asymptomatic older adult with a catheter is not performed, because the catheterised bladder is always colonised and the result leads to antibiotics that do no good.",
