            "<strong>Suppositories</strong> deliver a laxative (glycerol, bisacodyl) or a systemic drug (paracetamol, diclofenac, an antiemetic, an anticonvulsant in a fitting child) through the rectal mucosa. The patient is positioned as for an enema; the suppository is checked against the prescription, unwrapped and lubricated; the index finger is lubricated; the patient breathes out through the mouth; the suppository is inserted <strong>as the manufacturer directs</strong> — most are inserted pointed end first — and advanced <strong>past the internal anal sphincter so that it lies against the rectal wall</strong>, about a gloved finger's length (up to 10 cm) in an adult and about 5 cm in a child, 2.5 cm in an infant; a suppository pushed into a mass of faeces dissolves without reaching the mucosa and does nothing, so if the rectum is loaded an evacuant is given first; the buttocks are held together, and a laxative suppository is retained for <strong>fifteen to twenty minutes</strong> or as long as possible before the commode; a drug suppository is retained, and the patient is told not to expect a bowel action. Two suppositories are given one after the other, not together; a patient who can insert her own suppository is taught to do so.",
            "<strong>Digital rectal examination</strong> by a nurse trained and authorised in it establishes whether the rectum is loaded, the consistency of the stool, the tone of the sphincter, and whether an enema or suppository will reach anything; it is done with a lubricated gloved index finger inserted slowly with the patient breathing out, the finger swept around the rectal wall, and is never performed in the contraindications above. <strong>Manual (digital) evacuation of faeces</strong> is the last resort for impaction that oil retention enemas and suppositories have failed to clear, and a routine part of the bowel programme of some patients with a spinal cord injury; it is performed by a nurse trained in it, with the patient's consent, the pulse checked before and during, anaesthetic gel instilled and given time, the lubricated finger used to break up and hook out the stool a piece at a time with rests between, stopping for pain, bleeding, a fall in pulse rate or signs of autonomic dysreflexia; the result and the patient's response are recorded and the cause of the impaction is addressed so that it is not repeated. <strong>The flatus tube</strong> relieves painful gaseous distension after abdominal surgery, after an enema that produced flatus but no stool, and in some paralytic states: a lubricated soft rectal tube (22–30 French) is inserted <strong>10–15 cm</strong> with the patient in the left lateral position, its free end placed in a receiver of water under the bed so that escaping gas bubbles can be seen, and it is left in for <strong>no more than twenty to thirty minutes</strong> (a longer time damages the mucosa and the sphincter), removed, cleaned and repeated after two to three hours if needed; the abdomen is observed and measured, and persisting distension with vomiting and absent bowel sounds is reported as possible obstruction or ileus rather than treated with another tube.",
