            "<strong>Assessment and rehydration.</strong> Acute diarrhoea is three or more loose stools in twenty-four hours; it kills by dehydration and it spreads by hands, water and food. The patient is <strong>classified first</strong>: <em>no or mild dehydration</em> (alert, drinking normally, normal pulse and skin pinch) — oral rehydration solution after each stool at home (Plan A); <em>some dehydration</em> (restless or irritable, thirsty and drinking eagerly, sunken eyes, a skin pinch that returns slowly) — <strong>supervised oral rehydration, 75 mL/kg over four hours</strong> (Plan B), reassessed and continued; <em>severe dehydration</em> (lethargic or unconscious, unable to drink or drinking poorly, a very slow skin pinch, a weak or absent pulse, capillary refill over three seconds) — <strong>intravenous Ringer's lactate now</strong> (Plan C: 100 mL/kg, given as 30 mL/kg in the first thirty minutes for an adult or a child over one year (one hour for an infant) and 70 mL/kg over the next two and a half hours (five hours for an infant), with oral solution started as soon as the patient can drink), and a circulatory sign overrides the fact that the patient is still able to swallow. Low-osmolarity <strong>oral rehydration salts</strong> are made with the whole sachet in the stated volume of clean water, kept covered and discarded after twenty-four hours; <strong>zinc</strong> is given to every child under five (10 mg a day under six months, 20 mg from six months, for ten to fourteen days); feeding and breastfeeding continue; antidiarrhoeal drugs are not given to children and antibiotics are reserved for dysentery, cholera with severe dehydration and the immunocompromised. Output is charted by number and volume of stools, intake by the millilitre, and the weight repeated.",
            "<strong>Infection prevention and cholera.</strong> A patient with acute diarrhoea is nursed under <strong>contact precautions</strong> in a side room or a cohort bay with a dedicated toilet or commode, gloves and apron for every contact, and <strong>hand-washing with soap and water</strong> (alcohol rub does not reliably remove the spores of <em>Clostridioides difficile</em> or norovirus and is not relied on in cholera); stool and vomit are disinfected with <strong>2 per cent chlorine</strong> before disposal, surfaces, bedpans and commodes with 0.5 per cent, and linen is bagged separately. <strong>Suspected cholera</strong> — acute watery ‘rice-water’ diarrhoea with rapid dehydration in a person over two years in an area with cases, or any such death — is <strong>notified the same day</strong> to the district health directorate on the Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response system, a stool specimen or rectal swab goes in Cary–Blair medium, the patient is nursed on a cholera cot (a hole and a bucket with chlorine beneath) where output is large, oral rehydration is given continuously with intravenous Ringer's for severe cases, a single dose of an appropriate antibiotic is prescribed for severe dehydration, and the family is taught the barriers of the <strong>F-diagram</strong> — a latrine, hand-washing with soap after defecation and before food, safe water (boiled or chlorinated to 0.5 mg/L free chlorine at the point of use in an outbreak), covered food, and treatment of the household's water — because the outbreak that reached Greater Accra in late 2024 and spread to five regions by early 2025 killed mostly those who presented late after self-medication, and prevention is a nursing procedure too.",
